## Changed cases and inference limits

- **Stephen panel:** The fictional petitioner proposes chapel placement and kneeling in honour of Stephen, not paint. Calvin's reconstructed rejection concerns the devotional/church use, not a prohibited bodily depiction of God. Trent's different category account is not an actual installation licence. Removing the unsupported “unusual” condition does not repeal other requirements of the decree or assert this particular image meets them.
- **Household rescue sketch:** The family's drawing recalls neighbours' aid to the child, without prayer or religious honour. Calvin's visible-event distinction supports this limited application, not every domestic picture and not devotional church installation. The event and family remain invented.
- **Donor-labelled chip:** “John the Baptist — Amiens” is the invented donor's claim. The donor calls it bone; its material is not established. The clerk consults the already-earned catalogue because the name/place match the claim, not because the catalogue traces this chip. No chain of custody, ownership link, authentic relic, real submission, scientific examination or episcopal ruling is supplied.
- **Shorter comparison:** The existing Calvin source leaf already covers genuine as well as false relics. It now meets the earned Catholic hold directly. Further evidence and required recognition could change that fictional disposition; neither is claimed to exist. No invented authenticity exercise is necessary.

Underlying primary positions remain [Calvin, Institutes I.11 §§9–13](https://www.ccel.org/ccel/calvin/institutes.iii.xii.html), [Trent, Session XXV](https://history.hanover.edu/texts/trent/ct25.html), and Calvin's actual *Treatise on Relics*, printed pp.254–256, 276–278 in the [Krasinski translation](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/32136/pg32136-images.html). Translator preface/dissertation and notes are not Calvin. The two catalogue slips retain “Calvin reports”; no fraud finding or second independent witness is inferred.

